/**
 * A complete, self-contained synthetic scenario: one service day on one fictional network, with
 * its own events, demand sources, fixed core service, extra-bus pool, costs and assumptions.
 *
 * A scenario is the unit the review interface selects and the unit
 * {@link SimRunner#runComparison} evaluates. Every other record in the simulator is scoped by
 * `scenarioId`, so the shipped scenarios never interfere with one another and a new one can be
 * authored purely as data.
 *
 * **Reproducibility.** {@link SimScenario#randomSeed} plus the immutable
 * {@link SimDemandSnapshot} generated from it means every allocation size — and the mock
 * old-model baseline — is evaluated against identical passenger demand and identical realized
 * events. Re-running a scenario reproduces the previous numbers exactly.
 *
 * **Timezone handling.** All instants are stored as absolute UTC. {@link SimScenario#serviceDate}
 * and {@link SimScenario#serviceTimezone} exist so the UI can render local clock times and so
 * "which service day is this" is never inferred from a UTC timestamp.
 *
 * All scenarios are synthetic; {@link SimScenario#syntheticDataLabel} is the banner text the UI
 * shows to make that unmissable.
 */
entity type SimScenario {

  /**
   * What this scenario is designed to demonstrate — the capacity bottleneck, the event-ending
   * surge, the low-confidence report, and so on. Shown in the scenario picker.
   */
  description: string

  /** Service day in ISO `yyyy-MM-dd` form, in {@link SimScenario#serviceTimezone}. */
  serviceDate: !string

  /**
   * IANA timezone name for the service day, e.g. `America/Chicago`. Used only for display and for
   * interpreting {@link SimScenario#serviceDate}; the engine works entirely in absolute instants.
   */
  serviceTimezone: !string

  /**
   * First instant the simulation clock covers. Passenger arrivals before it are a validation
   * error rather than being silently dropped.
   */
  horizonStart: !datetime

  /**
   * Last instant the simulation clock covers.
   *
   * At the horizon the run stops and whatever remains is reported honestly: passengers still
   * queued appear in {@link SimAccounting#waitingAtHorizon}, passengers still riding in
   * {@link SimAccounting#onboardAtHorizon}. Nothing is extrapolated past this point.
   */
  horizonEnd: !datetime

  /**
   * The instant the daily plan is frozen — normally at or just before
   * {@link SimScenario#horizonStart}.
   *
   * {@link SimPlanner#buildPlan} may use **only** events with {@link SimEvent#knownAt} at or
   * before this instant. The simulator still realizes later events, so a candidate plan is
   * scored on how it copes with disruptions it could not have anticipated. There is no live
   * rerouting: the meeting rejected it, so the plan the simulator executes is exactly the plan
   * fixed here.
   */
  planningCutoff: !datetime

  /**
   * Seed for the deterministic PRNG used by demand generation (and nothing else). Identical seeds
   * give byte-identical {@link SimDemandSnapshot}s.
   *
   * Note that reuse of the *snapshot*, not merely of the seed, is what guarantees comparability
   * across allocations — different plans consume randomness in different orders, so sharing only
   * a seed would not be enough.
   */
  randomSeed: !int

  /**
   * Largest number of extra buses to evaluate. {@link SimRunner#runComparison} produces a
   * feasible daily plan for every allocation from 0 to this value inclusive, so the cost curve
   * shows the whole tradeoff rather than one recommended number.
   *
   * Capped by the number of {@link SimExtraBus} records actually available in the scenario.
   */
  maxExtraBuses: !int

  /** Cost accounting policy for this scenario; see {@link SimCostConfig}. */
  costConfig: SimCostConfig

  /** Every tunable modelling assumption for this scenario; see {@link SimAssumptions}. */
  assumptions: SimAssumptions

  /**
   * Version of the simulator data contracts this scenario was authored against, e.g.
   * `sim-contract-1.0.0`. Echoed into {@link SimulationInput#contractVersion} and every export so
   * a consumer can tell which shape it is reading.
   */
  contractVersion: string

  /**
   * Human-readable synthetic-data banner, e.g. "Synthetic mock data — not MTD operational data".
   * Rendered prominently by the review UI and included in exports.
   */
  syntheticDataLabel: string

  /** Short tags used to group and filter scenarios in the picker, e.g. `rush`, `surge`, `fixture`. */
  scenarioTags: [string]

  /** Always true — synthetic mock data. */
  isSynthetic: boolean
}
